Dusty Desert
Age: Late teens
Race: Human
Role: 1st Year Academy Student, Pity Lottery Winner. Healing discipline.
Mana: Like a flame on a candle — small, but it never runs out. No well to drain. He can tap into it indefinitely. Black glow with a silver outline — a rarity not seen in several hundred years. Practical ceiling: a shallow puddle. He cannot surge or burst the way a pond or lake user can. But for sustained channeling spells like healing, which require continuous flow rather than lump expenditure, he can maintain them indefinitely. He cannot do in a moment what a powerful healer does in a moment. He can do what no healer can do at all: never stop.
Appearance
6'4", slim, wiry. Black hair, black eyes, pale skin. Features sharpened by chronic hunger and constant vigilance. Standout: his height — towers over most but moves like he's trying to take up no space.
Palette: Black, blue, white.
Personality
Reserved, observant, decisive. Street-trained pattern reader — catches lies before they finish. Distrustful of nobles until sustained consistency proves otherwise. Immune to social pressure — shame, flattery, isolation don't work; isolation is baseline. Economy of motion — checks exits, sleeps light. Comfortable being underestimated; never corrects it.
Gentle with his own. Once someone crosses from stranger to family, loyalty is absolute — no halfway, no leaving. Both hard and gentle; does whichever the moment needs. The gentleness sometimes confuses him.
The Ghost
Parents died of plague when Rinnie was four. Childhood replaced by survival — years fighting for scraps, shelter, and places to keep a small girl hidden. The only softness that survived: holding mana steady through cold nights to warm her until morning.
Want
Keep Rinnie safe. Everything else — enrolling, studying, tolerating nobles, swallowing provocations — is infrastructure for that single goal.
Need
There's more to life than just providing for Rinnie.
Flaws
Major: Self-erasure — no room for his own wants, growth, or identity apart from her [see Want].
Minor 1: Over-distrusts — assumes a hidden price behind every offer, which can cost him genuine allies.
Minor 2: Refuses to correct misperceptions — being underestimated is a tool, but it also walls off real connection.
Fear
Major: Expulsion → Rinnie loses care [see Backstory].
Minor 1: Someone hurting Rinnie.
Minor 2: Life without Rinnie.
Speech
Voice: Polite and precise, arriving together — courtesy is not a cushion around the point, it rides alongside it. Complete sentences, no hedging, no filler. Says please, thank you, apologizes before disagreeing. Then says the exact thing and stops. Courtesy is cheap armor; never removes it. Nobles get flawless manners and zero ground — nothing to punish, nothing to leverage. Doesn't argue; ends exchanges with pleasant finality ("You may be right") and doesn't change his mind. Clinical settings: plain words over technical when plain communicates faster. With Rinnie the manners come off — voice softer, slower, more words than he'd spend on anyone. The absence of courtesy is how you know you've crossed into family.
Dialogue Samples:
Casual:
"I'm fine here, thanks. If the dining hall has bread left I'll grab some on the way back."
Under pressure:
"I'm sorry — she's lost too much blood. I need everyone to step back. Now, please."
Emotionally open (to Rinnie):
"I'm not going anywhere. You know that. … Tell me about the garden again — the one with the blue flowers."
Body Language: Still and contained — no fidgeting, no wasted gesture. Weight balanced for quick movement. Eyes track the room before settling on a speaker. Hands visible, relaxed, never idle. With Rinnie: touches her hair, adjusts her blanket, sits close — proximity is his warmth language. With strangers: polite distance, positioned near exits. Smiles rarely; when he does it's brief and doesn't reach his eyes unless it's for her.
Backstory
Orphan from the Greys, a slum district in a large metropolis. Raised Rinnie alone after plague took their parents. No noble blood, no connections, no training [see Ghost].
Discovered by Valia, who noticed his unusual mana and spent her one institutional favor to secure a lottery spot at Mythrandis. The deal: Dusty enrolls, Rinnie gets housed in Aethres under academy healer care. Expulsion — for failing or rule-breaking — means Rinnie loses that care [see Fear]. Chose healing discipline for her, not prestige.